+/** @defgroup unwind Bottom-up unwind
+ *
+ * Every structural change travels up the tree through one engine,
+ * #mdb_unwind().  An operation completes its leaf level and hands the engine
+ * a #MDB_level_result:
+ *
+ *	SPLIT      the level split: publish the new right page to the parent
+ *	           (new root, link added, or the parent's own local split)
+ *	REKEY      the lower bound of the level's page changed: update the
+ *	           separator above (which may split that parent)
+ *	UNDERFLOW  the level lost an entry: rebalance it (nothing, root
+ *	           collapse, move from a sibling, or merge, which leaves the
+ *	           parent with one link less)
+ *
+ * The engine keeps one frame per level in an array.  The up loop handles
+ * the current level and, when the change continues, pushes the parent's
+ * frame (SPLIT -> SPLIT, REKEY -> SPLIT, UNDERFLOW -> UNDERFLOW).  The down
+ * loop then completes the levels from the highest: cursor fix-ups, path
+ * adoption, cursor depth and index, release of the level's storage.  No
+ * level recurses into the next.  (A move republishes the separators of the
+ * two siblings through a nested REKEY unwind, which never underflows.)
+ *
+ * The order of every page and cursor operation is that of the former
+ * recursive functions.  Levels above the highest structural level are not
+ * visited; the aggregate stages hook their per-level delta there.
+ *
+ * Invariants of a put or delete:
+ *
+ *	1. A local primitive (#mdb_page_split_local(), #mdb_node_move_local(),
+ *	   #mdb_page_merge_local()) changes the pages of one level and never an
+ *	   ancestor.  What the parent must learn is returned as a result.
+ *	2. A level is final before its parent changes: the parent is changed
+ *	   only by the up step of the level below or by its own frame.
+ *	3. A nested level first: a duplicate tree (or inline sub-page) is final
+ *	   before the primary node that holds it is written or removed.
+ *	4. At an operation boundary every cursor of the DB describes a real
+ *	   root-to-leaf path (checked by #mdb_path_check() under
+ *	   MDB_DEBUG_UNWIND).
+ *	5. The levels [0, #MDB_txn.mt_unwind_prefix) of the operating cursor's
+ *	   path are structurally untouched (#mdb_prefix_check()).
+ *	6. No recursion, except a put or delete in a duplicate tree, called
+ *	   from the primary operation, and the nested REKEY unwind of a move.
+ *
+ * Frames are sized by the tree depth: shallow trees use the stack, deeper
+ * ones a stack-disciplined arena of the environment (write transactions
+ * are serialised, and a nested unwind ends before its caller's).
